<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarahan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarahan is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, high above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> valley. It was the summer seat of the Rajas of Bashahr, whose palace stands there. Its Bhimakali temple, built in a distinctive Himalayan style of wood and stone, was the family shrine of the dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarahan.jpg" alt="Sarahan" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sanyam Bahga · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kinnaur_048.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sarahan lies at an average elevation of 2,313 metres (7,589 feet), about 172 kilometres by road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> and 564 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>. The river Satluj flows seven kilometres below it, or 17 kilometres by road. From November to March the region receives moderate to heavy snowfall. Traces of the old Indo-Tibetan Road, along which trade passed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the Tibetan regions, still run through Shalabag near Sarahan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sarahan Bushahr was the summer capital of the kingdom of Bushahr, while Rampur Bushahr served as its winter capital. The kings of Bushahr are believed to have been the dynastic priests of the Bhimakali Temple, originally known as the Bhimadevi Temple, and lived within its premises before moving to a palace about 100 metres away. The temple houses the kuldevi, the presiding deity of the dynasty, the mother goddess Bhimakali, and is counted among the 51 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shakta</a> pithas.</p><p>The temple holds both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Vajrayana Buddhist statues and decorations, which reflect the trade that passed this way between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, and some of its stone images may date to the Kushan era, from about the 1st to the 3rd centuries CE. It is built in the Kath-Kuni style of the hills: alternate rows of grooved and interlocked stones and wood give the walls their strength, and thick walls under low roofs keep the buildings warm in winter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Two temple buildings stand side by side, one old and restored and the other relatively new. The King's and Queen's Palace beside the temple are comparatively recent buildings; the royal family does not live there but visits during the Durga Puja festival in October.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarahan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early capital was at Kamru in Kinnaur, and later rulers moved between <b>Sarahan</b> and Rampur on the Sutlej.</blockquote><a href="/state/bashahr-state">Bashahr State</a></li></ul></section>
